+# NIST identifies an original steel-selection guide
+
+**STL01–STL03. Source:** [NIST NCSTAR 1-3B, Steel Inventory and Identification](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/NCSTAR/ncstar1-3b.pdf), September 2005, physical PDF p. 25 (printed p. xxiii), and p. 31 (printed p. 3). The official PDF was acquired on 2026-11-15 and SHA-256 checked: `d234d025912dab100a0dd479f826b2adff0fc23acd5acb42efc93a4a8bcd9bc3`. Both pages were visually inspected; the full guide was not acquired or read. A preserved copy of the PDF, also previously used in other reviews, remains in the research media store.
+
+**Observation.** The acknowledgments name David Sharp as coordinator of volunteer activity and author of a guide used to select suitable pieces. They also describe Ramon Gilsanz as leader of the recovery effort and Audrey Massa of FEMA as leader for documenting and cataloging the effort and pieces to be saved. These are NIST's descriptions of roles, not independent authentication of the guide's version, distribution or use on any particular item. [Acknowledgments, PDF p. 25](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/NCSTAR/ncstar1-3b.pdf#page=25)
+
+**Separate observation.** The report's recovery-background chapter attributes a list of searched-for categories to McAllister (2002). Among them are badly burned pieces from WTC 7, connections from WTC 1, 2 and 7, bolts, and floor trusses. That is NIST's later summary; the page does not identify this list as the guide, reproduce the guide, or establish what was recovered in each category. [Recovery background, PDF p. 31](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/NCSTAR/ncstar1-3b.pdf#page=31)
+
+**Why it matters.** The guide is a concrete original-record lead for testing how the selection criteria were expressed, changed, communicated and applied. The acknowledgment supports that a guide existed as reported by NIST; it does not establish its exact title, date, contents, distribution, completeness, or application to W14A, W14B or any WTC 7 specimen. No discrepancy between the missing guide and the chapter's list can be assessed without the guide. Selection decisions and later building attribution are distinct questions. This is not evidence of wrongdoing or concealment.
+
